{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["And the reason of <b>I have lifted up my hand to YHWH</b> is in the language of an oath: \"I lift up my hand to El Elyon,\" and likewise <b>בי נשבעתי</b> (Genesis 22:16) — \"I have sworn\"; this is the language of Rashi. And likewise I found in the Sifre: \"We find in all the righteous that they adjure their impulse not to transgress; concerning Abraham it says: 'I have lifted up my hand to YHWH.'\" And this is like the language of \"And he lifted up his right hand and his left hand to the heavens and swore by the Living One of the World\" (Daniel 12:7). And Onkelos said: <b>ארימית ידי בצלו קדם ה'</b> — that is, \"I prayed to YHWH and my hands were outstretched to the heavens: if I take from all that is yours,\" meaning, \"may Elohim do so to me and may He add more so, if I take.\" And what seems correct in my eyes is that he said, \"I have lifted up my hand to YHWH,\" to consecrate and dedicate it before Him if I take from all that is yours, for consecrations are called by this term — <b>תרומת יד</b> — as in <b>כל מרים תרומת כסף ונחשת</b> (Exodus 35:24), <b>וכל איש אשר הניף תנופת זהב לה'</b> (Exodus 35:22). And he said this because he gave a tithe from it, for whatever he would take from all that was his would become a תרומה to YHWH — he would derive no benefit from it at all. And in Bereshit Rabbah (43:9): \"He made it a תרומה, as it says, <b>והרימותם ממנו תרומת ה'</b> (Numbers 18:26).\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:29:25.642897107Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}